  "items": [
    {
      "id": "https://deepmind.com/alphagenome-3",
      "url": "https://deepmind.com/alphagenome-3",
      "title": "AlphaGenome 3: AI-driven whole-genome design \u2014 10Mbp context + variant editing",
      "content_html": "<p>AlphaGenome 3 is DeepMind&#x27;s 3rd-generation whole-genome model. The v3 release adds generative genome design: given a target phenotype (e.g. &#x27;drought-resistant rice&#x27;), AlphaGenome 3 designs a complete genome (gene sequences + regulatory elements + chromatin structure) that produces the target phenotype with 78% accuracy.</p><p>Key upgrade: 10Mbp context window (vs v2&#x27;s 1Mbp) \u2014 long enough to capture entire gene clusters + topologically-associating domains (TADs) + enhancer-promoter loops in a single prediction. The model is 200B params (MoE 128 experts, top-4 routing).</p><p>On the GenoDesign-2027 benchmark (50 target phenotypes \u00d7 5 species), AlphaGenome 3 achieves 78% phenotype-accuracy (vs v2&#x27;s 61%). 23 of 50 designed genomes have been synthesized + tested in vivo \u2014 18 show the target phenotype.</p>",
      "summary": "AlphaGenome 3 is DeepMind's 3rd-generation whole-genome model. The v3 release adds generative genome design: given a target phenotype (e.g. 'drought-resistant rice'), AlphaGenome 3 designs a complete genome (gene sequences + regulatory elements + chromatin structure) that produces the target phenotype with 78% accuracy.",
      "date_published": "2027-01-28T00:00:00Z",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "DeepMind Genomics"
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "Genomics - Genome design",
        "DeepMind blog"
      ],
      "_aihpc_dataset": {
        "name": "GenoDesign-2027 benchmark (50 phenotypes \u00d7 5 species)",
        "format": "FASTA + JSON",
        "size": "4.2 GB",
        "url": "https://github.com/alphagenome/3-2027"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "https://blogs.nvidia.com/cosmos",
      "url": "https://blogs.nvidia.com/cosmos",
      "title": "NVIDIA Cosmos: 700B-param world foundation model \u2014 understands + generates any physical reality",
      "content_html": "<p>Cosmos is NVIDIA&#x27;s 700B-parameter (MoE 256 experts, top-8 routing) world foundation model. It understands + generates any physical reality: from molecular dynamics to climate, from robotics to game worlds. The model is trained on 10B video clips + 1B physics simulations + 100M 3D meshes.</p><p>On WorldBench (50 tasks across 5 modalities: video prediction, 3D generation, physics simulation, robotics control, game-world creation), Cosmos achieves 0.87 \u2014 the first model to exceed 0.85 on all modalities simultaneously. The key innovation: a unified &#x27;physics-token&#x27; representation that encodes physical laws (conservation, causality, locality) as token embeddings.</p><p>Released as Cosmos API (cloud) + Cosmos Edge (70B, on RTX 5090). Used in 47 game studios for world generation + 23 robotics companies for sim-to-real + 12 climate institutions for weather visualisation.</p>",
      "summary": "Cosmos is NVIDIA's 700B-parameter (MoE 256 experts, top-8 routing) world foundation model. It understands + generates any physical reality: from molecular dynamics to climate, from robotics to game worlds. The model is trained on 10B video clips + 1B physics simulations + 100M 3D meshes.",
      "date_published": "2027-01-30T00:00:00Z",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "NVIDIA Research"
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "CS - World foundation model",
        "NVIDIA blog"
      ],
      "_aihpc_dataset": {
        "name": "WorldBench (50 tasks \u00d7 5 modalities)",
        "format": "MP4 + OBJ + JSON",
        "size": "8.4 GB",
        "url": "https://github.com/nvidia/cosmos-2027"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "https://anthropic.com/claude-5-max",
      "url": "https://anthropic.com/claude-5-max",
      "title": "Claude 5 Max: 10T-param reasoning model with 100M-token thinking \u2014 solves Riemann fully",
      "content_html": "<p>Claude 5 Max is Anthropic&#x27;s 10-trillion-parameter (MoE 4096 experts, top-32 routing) ultra-reasoning model. The &#x27;Max&#x27; variant extends Claude 5 Ultra&#x27;s thinking budget from 10M to 100M tokens \u2014 enabling week-long deductive reasoning. Active params: ~250B per token.</p><p>On the Riemann Hypothesis Challenge (RH-2027), Claude 5 Max completes the proof that Claude 5 Ultra could not \u2014 passing all 10/10 verification checks. The key: the 100M-token budget allows Claude 5 Max to prove the L-function conjecture that was the remaining gap. If peer-reviewed, this would be the first AI-solved Millennium Prize problem.</p><p>On ARC-AGI-2, Claude 5 Max scores 0.95 \u2014 matching the human-expert threshold. On METR (100-step agent tasks), 73%. Released via API; cost: $5000 per 1M thinking tokens (100M \u00d7 $5000 = $500K per proof attempt).</p>",
      "summary": "Claude 5 Max is Anthropic's 10-trillion-parameter (MoE 4096 experts, top-32 routing) ultra-reasoning model. The 'Max' variant extends Claude 5 Ultra's thinking budget from 10M to 100M tokens \u2014 enabling week-long deductive reasoning. Active params: ~250B per token.",
      "date_published": "2027-02-01T00:00:00Z",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Anthropic Research"
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "CS - Ultra reasoning",
        "Anthropic blog"
      ],
      "_aihpc_dataset": {
        "name": "RH-2027 full proof + ARC-AGI-2 results",
        "format": "LaTeX + JSON",
        "size": "180 MB",
        "url": "https://github.com/anthropic/claude-5-max-2027"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "https://ai.meta.com/esm-7",
      "url": "https://ai.meta.com/esm-7",
      "title": "ESM-7: 1T-param protein design model \u2014 82% first-try foldable + novel enzyme classes",
      "content_html": "<p>ESM-7 is Meta&#x27;s 1-trillion-parameter (MoE 256 experts, top-8) protein design model. The v7 release pushes the first-try foldable rate to 82% (vs ESM-6&#x27;s 68%, ESM-3&#x27;s 18% in 2024 \u2014 4.5\u00d7 improvement in 3 years). The curve is approaching saturation.</p><p>Key innovation: ESM-7 can design novel enzyme classes that don&#x27;t exist in nature. On the EnzymeBench-2027 (50 novel enzyme targets), ESM-7 produces 31/50 with measurable catalytic activity (vs ESM-6&#x27;s 23/50). 7 of these are entirely novel fold architectures not seen in the PDB \u2014 the first AI to invent new protein folds.</p><p>Released under CC-BY-NC 4.0. The 1T-param MoE means only ~80B active per token \u2014 deployable on a single NVIDIA B200 (192 GB HBM3e).</p>",
      "summary": "ESM-7 is Meta's 1-trillion-parameter (MoE 256 experts, top-8) protein design model. The v7 release pushes the first-try foldable rate to 82% (vs ESM-6's 68%, ESM-3's 18% in 2024 \u2014 4.5\u00d7 improvement in 3 years). The curve is approaching saturation.",
      "date_published": "2027-01-29T00:00:00Z",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Meta AI FAIR"
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "Biology - Protein FM",
        "Meta AI blog"
      ],
      "_aihpc_dataset": {
        "name": "EnzymeBench-2027 (50 novel enzyme targets)",
        "format": "FASTA + PDB + CSV",
        "size": "1.8 GB",
        "url": "https://github.com/evolutionaryscale/esm-7-2027"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "https://psiquantum.com/bitternut",
      "url": "https://psiquantum.com/bitternut",
      "title": "PsiQuantum Bitternut: 1-million-qubit photonic quantum computer with fault-tolerant operation",
      "content_html": "<p>PsiQuantum Bitternut is a 1-million-qubit photonic quantum computer using single-photon sources + linear optical circuits + cryogenic single-photon detectors. The photonic approach avoids the cryogenic dilution refrigerators of superconducting systems \u2014 the qubits are photons at room temperature; only the detectors are cryogenic (100mK).</p><p>Bitternut runs a distance-13 surface code (169 data + 168 ancilla = 337 qubits per logical) achieving 10^-6 logical error rate. The 1M physical qubits support ~2000 logical qubits \u2014 sufficient for Shor RSA-4096 (estimated 50M logical-qubit-cycles, ~8 hours).</p><p>Manufactured by GlobalFoundries on 300mm silicon photonic wafers. Available via PsiQuantum Cloud. The photonic approach enables room-temperature operation + inherent networking (photons can be sent through optical fibre between quantum computers).</p>",
      "summary": "PsiQuantum Bitternut is a 1-million-qubit photonic quantum computer using single-photon sources + linear optical circuits + cryogenic single-photon detectors. The photonic approach avoids the cryogenic dilution refrigerators of superconducting systems \u2014 the qubits are photons at room temperature; only the detectors are cryogenic (100mK).",
      "date_published": "2027-01-25T00:00:00Z",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "PsiQuantum + GlobalFoundries"
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "Physics - Photonic quantum computing",
        "PsiQuantum blog"
      ],
      "_aihpc_dataset": {
        "name": "Bitternut QEC benchmark (d=7,9,11,13) + Shor RSA-4096 estimate",
        "format": "CSV + Qiskit shots",
        "size": "480 MB",
        "url": "https://github.com/psiquantum/bitternut-2027"
      }
    },
